  "body": "It is not clear to me what should happen if we have:\r\n```javascript\r\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {\r\n  elem.style.order = 1;\r\n  Promise.resolve().then(() => {\r\n    elem.style.order = 2;\r\n  });\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nWould this cause one reflow or two?\r\n\r\nIt seems to me that Gecko delays the microtask after the layout flush (probably delayed to the next loop) so two reflows, while Blink runs the microtask immediately after the animation frame callback, so only one reflow is triggered.\r\n\r\nAnother observable difference:\r\n```javascript\r\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {\r\n  console.log('rAF 1');\r\n  Promise.resolve().then(() => {\r\n    console.log('promise in rAF 1');\r\n  });\r\n});\r\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {\r\n  console.log('rAF 2');\r\n  Promise.resolve().then(() => {\r\n    console.log('promise in rAF 2');\r\n  });\r\n});\r\n```\r\nGecko does:\r\n* rAF 1\r\n* rAF 2\r\n* promise in rAF 1\r\n* promise in rAF 2\r\n\r\nwhile Blink does:\r\n* rAF 1\r\n* promise in rAF 1\r\n* rAF 2\r\n* promise in rAF 2\r\n\r\nIt seems to me that there is no microtask checkpoint in \"run the animation frame callbacks\", so maybe Gecko's behavior is more conformant? But I also have the feeling that Blink's behavior might be more intuitive?",
